; ports/process — leaving, address space, and signals.
;
; NOT named `ports/proc`: E155 makes two different modules under one basename a
; hard error in both resolvers, and `scaffold/lib/proc.chiral` already owns the
; key `proc`. A module's key is its basename, not its path.
;
; leave: exit is the clean end, halt is the fatal alarm counter effect
; (docs/error-and-alarm.md). Both are polymorphic so an alarm path can close
; what it holds and leave without faking an inhabitant of its return type.

(import "prelude/prelude")

(extern exit (-> (0 A (type 0)) (=> I64 A)))
(extern halt (-> (0 A (type 0)) (=> Str A)))

(extern mmap (=> I64 I64 I64 I64 I64 I64 I64))  ; mmap(addr,len,prot,flags,fd,off) -> base or -errno
(extern mprotect (=> I64 I64 I64 I64))            ; mprotect(addr,len,prot) -> 0 or -errno
(extern signal (=> I64 I64 I64))              ; signal(signum,handler) -> old handler (SIG_DFL=0/SIG_IGN=1)

; heap-allocated: the arena's bump cursor, read out. `heapptr - heapbase` is
; TOTAL BYTES EVER ALLOCATED, exactly -- the cursor only advances and nothing
; reclaims, so it is the allocator's own state and not an inference from RSS.
; The four arena cells are stored by the entry stub before main runs
; (lib/lowering/compile-emit.chiral:36-44); this crossing reads two of them.
;
; Why `=>`, and why this registry. The answer depends on mutable machine state:
; the same argument gives a different answer once the program has allocated.
; Prelude's standing invariant is that every extern it declares is `->`
; (prelude.chiral:10 -- "every one of them is `->`, so its `crossings` is empty
; by derivation"), so an effectful extern has no home there. An effectful extern
; is a bound crossing by the tree's own derivation (module/loader.chiral
; crossings-of), and MAP.md puts a declared crossing under ports/. It joins
; mmap/mprotect on THIS registry instead of minting a tenth: the arena's own
; crossings are already here, and ports.chiral argues against a registry per
; single declaration.
(extern heap-allocated (=> Unit I64))
